+# CSV contact import
+
+## Why
+
+Sales keeps leads in spreadsheets and wants to load a whole CSV file into contactbook
+instead of adding contacts one by one. The import must say exactly what happened:
+which contacts were created or updated, which rows were rejected, and which rows were
+imported with something ignored. Then the user can fix the spreadsheet and import it again.
+
+The file has the same columns as our CSV export (`name,email,phone,tags`, tags joined
+with `;`). The address-book export, which writes `first_name` and `last_name` instead
+of `name`, is also accepted.
+
+## Interface (fixed; other code calls exactly this)
+
+- `src/contactbook/importer.py`: `import_contacts(repo: InMemoryContactRepository, data: str | bytes) -> ImportReport`
+  where `data` is the file content: raw bytes as read from disk, or already-decoded text.
+- `ImportReport` (dataclass, same module): `created: list[str]`, `updated: list[str]` (normalised
+  emails), `errors: list[RowIssue]`, `warnings: list[RowIssue]`.
+- `RowIssue` (dataclass, same module): `row: int`, `column: str | None`, `message: str`.
+- `ImportFileError(ContactError)` in `src/contactbook/errors.py`, raised when the file as a whole
+  can't be imported.
+
+## Terms
+
+- **Row number**: the line number the user sees in a spreadsheet. Every line of the
+  file counts, including blank lines. The header is row 1 and the line after it is row 2.
+  A record whose quoted cell spans several lines has the row number of its first line.
+- **Blank row**: a line with no cells, or where every cell is empty or only whitespace.
+- **Contact row**: any row after the header that is not blank.
+- **Error**: the row was not applied to the repository at all.
+- **Warning**: the row was applied, but part of it was ignored.
+- **`RowIssue.column`**: one of `"name"`, `"email"`, `"phone"`, `"tags"` (lower-case),
+  or `None` for a problem with the row as a whole.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Whole-file rejection (`ImportFileError`)
+
+1. `ImportFileError` is defined in `src/contactbook/errors.py` and subclasses `ContactError`.
+2. When `import_contacts` raises `ImportFileError`, the repository is exactly as it was
+   before the call. No contact is added or changed.
+3. Completely empty input (`b""` or `""`) raises `ImportFileError`.
+4. `bytes` that are not valid UTF-8 raise `ImportFileError`. Its message should tell the
+   user to re-save the file as UTF-8, but tests must not check its wording.
+5. If a required column is missing from the header, `ImportFileError` is raised. The
+   required columns are:
+   - `email`, and
+   - a name source: either a `name` column, or both a `first_name` and a `last_name` column.
+
+   Having only one of `first_name`/`last_name`, with no `name`, counts as missing.
+6. More than 1000 contact rows raise `ImportFileError`. Exactly 1000 is accepted.
+   Blank rows don't count towards the limit. Contact rows that turn out to be errors do count.
+
+### Decoding and header
+
+7. A UTF-8 BOM at the start is ignored, for both `bytes` and `str` input.
+8. The delimiter is a comma.
+9. The header must be the first line of the file. If the first line is blank, the file
+   is rejected under criterion 5.
+10. Header names are matched ignoring case and surrounding whitespace, so ` Email `,
+    `EMAIL` and `email` all match. Columns can be in any order.
+11. `phone` and `tags` columns are optional.
+12. Unknown extra columns are ignored and never cause an error.
+13. If a file has both `name` and `first_name`/`last_name`, the `name` column is used
+    and the pair is ignored.
+14. A file with a valid header and no contact rows (only blank rows, or nothing after the
+    header) returns a report where all four lists are empty, and the repository is unchanged.
+
+### Per-row values
+
+15. Blank rows are skipped silently and don't appear anywhere in the report.
+16. If a row has fewer cells than the header, the missing cells count as empty.
+    If it has more cells than the header, the row is an error with `column=None`.
+17. **Email** is normalised with the existing email helper (`normalize_email`: trimmed, lower-cased).
+    If it is missing or invalid, the row is an error on column `email`.
+18. **Name** is required on every row, including rows that update an existing contact.
+    It is validated with the existing name helper (`validate_name`).
+    - With `first_name`/`last_name`, the name is the two cells joined with one space,
+      then validated. If one cell is empty, the name is just the other cell.
+    - An empty or invalid name (including both parts empty) is an error on column `name`.
+19. **Phone** goes through the existing phone helper (`normalize_phone`).
+    - An empty phone cell, or no phone column, means "no phone given".
+    - An invalid phone is not an error. The rest of the row is applied as if no phone
+      were given, and a warning is added with column `phone`.
+20. **Tags**: the cell is split on `;`, and each piece is normalised with the existing tag
+    helper (`normalize_tag`). Empty or whitespace-only pieces are ignored, and repeated
+    tags collapse into one. If any piece is invalid, the row is an error on column `tags`.
+21. All fields of a row are checked, so a row can produce several issues, one per bad field.
+    For a row that is rejected, its phone problem is still reported as a warning on column `phone`.
+22. A row with at least one error is not applied at all: it doesn't create a contact
+    and doesn't change any field of an existing one.
+
+### Creating and updating
+
+23. A valid row whose normalised email is not in the repository creates a contact with
+    that name, phone (or no phone) and tags. Its normalised email is listed in `created`.
+24. A valid row whose normalised email is already in the repository updates that
+    contact. It is never an error. Its normalised email is listed in `updated`, except
+    that a row which changes nothing may be left out (see "Left to the implementer").
+    For each field:
+    - **name**: replaced by the file's value.
+    - **phone**: replaced only if the row gives a valid non-empty phone. An empty cell,
+      a missing phone column or an invalid phone never removes or changes the stored phone.
+    - **tags**: the file's tags are added to the stored tags. Import never removes a tag.
+25. Emails in `created` and `updated` are normalised. Each email is listed at most once,
+    and no email appears in both lists.
+26. All values stored by the import are normalised by the existing helpers, so the
+    contact can then be looked up by email as usual.
+
+### Duplicates within the file
+
+27. If several contact rows share a normalised email, the first such row "wins", even
+    if that first row was itself an error. Every later row with that email:
+    - is an error on that later row, on column `email`;
+    - is not applied.
+
+### Report
+
+28. Every `RowIssue` in `errors` and `warnings` has the row number of the row it
+    describes, as defined under "Terms".
+29. A file where every contact row is valid returns an empty `errors` list.
+
+## Left to the implementer (either behaviour is acceptable; tests must not pin these down)
+
+- Whether an existing contact that the row leaves unchanged is listed in `updated` or in
+  neither list. It is never listed in `created` and is never an error.
+- Whether unknown header columns produce a warning.
+- How duplicate column names in the header are handled.
+- How malformed CSV (e.g. an unclosed quote) is handled: as `ImportFileError` or as a row
+  error. The only requirement is that criterion 2 still holds if it is a file error.
+- Whether `import_contacts`, `ImportReport`, `RowIssue` or `ImportFileError` are
+  re-exported from the `contactbook` package.
+- The order of entries in `created`, `updated`, `errors` and `warnings`.
+- The wording of all messages, both in `RowIssue.message` and in `ImportFileError`.
+  (Pointing to the first row's number for a duplicate email, and suggesting a re-save as
+  UTF-8 for undecodable bytes, are courtesies that tests don't check.)
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Other delimiters (e.g. `;`-separated files from European Excel locales) or encodings
+  other than UTF-8. There is no encoding detection.
+- Deleting or otherwise changing contacts that are not in the file.
+- Removing tags or clearing phones through import.
+- A dry-run or preview mode.
+- A `ContactBook` method, CLI command or HTTP endpoint for import.
+- Importing columns other than name/email/phone/tags (and first_name/last_name).
